              data-article-section="screen"
            >
              <header class="color-section__header">
                <span class="color-section__index">01</span>
                <div>
                  <p class="color-section__eyebrow">Perception</p>
                  <h2>Screen colour is not pigment</h2>
                </div>
              </header>

              <p>
                The same colour code passes through three completely different
                physical systems before it reaches a finished pin, and each one
                changes the result in its own way.
              </p>

              <div class="color-render">
                {renderRows.map((row, index) => (
                  <div class="color-render__item">
                    <span class="color-render__num">0{index + 1}</span>
                    <h3>{row.label}</h3>
                    <p>{row.note}</p>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <p>
                A screen emits light; enamel reflects it. A screen has a fixed
                white point and a fixed gamma; the room around you does not. And
                between the screen and the finished part sits a metal body whose
                own colour is part of the result: the same enamel over brass, over
                zinc alloy and over aluminium is not the same enamel, and a light
                or pastel mix will show the substrate far more than a dark one
                does.
              </p>

              <p>
                This is not a supplier failing to try hard enough. It is a
                mismatch between two media, and no amount of care closes it. What
                care can do is move the agreement onto something that does not
                depend on either medium — which is where the Pantone code comes
                in.
              </p>

              <div class="color-note">
                <p class="color-note__label">Why this page has no colour swatches</p>
                <p class="color-note__foot">
                  A swatch on this page would be rendered by your screen, in your
                  room, at your brightness — the exact conditions the article says
                  cannot be trusted. The diagrams here show structure and
                  sequence, never colour. Any colour judgement has to happen on a
                  chip and on a cured sample.
                </p>
              </div>
            </section>

            <!-- ---------- 02 ---------- -->
            <section id="file" class="color-section" data-article-section="file">
