                    <span class="color-step__num">{step.num}</span>
                    <div>
                      <h3>{step.title}</h3>
                      <p>{step.text}</p>
                    </div>
                  </div>
                ))}
              </div>

              <p>
                The code is a reference, not a recipe, and being honest about
                that is what makes it usable. A Pantone chip is ink on coated
                paper. Enamel is pigment in a binder over metal. They can be
                brought close, and a good mixing bench brings them very close —
                but the code fixes the intention, while the substrate, the enamel
                thickness and the finish decide the result.
              </p>

              <p>
                Three practical consequences follow. Codes in the metallic range
                cannot be reproduced as flat enamel at all — they need a metallic
                base or a plated field, and a proof should say so rather than
                quietly substituting. Pastel and light mixes sit closer to the
                substrate and shift more than dark ones. And a chip that has been
                sitting in a window for a year is no longer a reference, which is
                why the book gets replaced rather than trusted indefinitely.
              </p>
